From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 21:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: flush the write buffers by waiting them out on the PS5

NtFlushProcessWriteBuffers was a stub on the console: no membarrier, and a program that relies on it (the .NET
garbage collector does) could see another thread's stores out of order; kernel32's store buffer litmus test counted
dozens of reordered stores. The page protection trick CoreCLR uses where it has no membarrier was tried and
removed: it made a million mprotect calls in half a minute, ended the title, and the console dropped off the network.
This CPU now fences and waits 5 microseconds, far longer than a store takes to leave its store buffer, which gives
every thread that reads after the call the stores that came before it. It touches no kernel interface.
---
  dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
  1 file changed, 31 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
index 4fc7dea..03af6f6 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -7915,6 +7915,37 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI NtFlushProcessWriteBuffers(void)
     return STATUS_SUCCESS;
 }
 
+#elif defined(__PROSPERO__)
+
+/**********************************************************************
+ *           NtFlushProcessWriteBuffers  (NTDLL.@)
+ *
+ * The console has no membarrier and no safe way to interrupt the CPUs that run the process's other threads (changing
+ * the protection of a dirty page, as CoreCLR does where it has no membarrier, ran the call about a million times in a
+ * kernel32 test, ended the title and the console dropped off the network). On x86 a store reaches the other CPUs when
+ * its store buffer drains, which takes tens to hundreds of nanoseconds and does not depend on anything the other CPU
+ * does; so this CPU fences, and waits for much longer than that. A thread whose store came before this call is then
+ * seen by every thread that reads after it, which is all that FlushProcessWriteBuffers promises: if the caller reads
+ * a location another thread wrote just before (and that thread read the caller's flag before the caller's store was
+ * visible), the wait is longer than the two drains the order needs. It relies on that bound. kernel32's store buffer
+ * litmus test passes with it, and passed on the host with no wait at all (only the fence), so the test cannot tell the
+ * wait from the fence: the wait rests on the argument, not on the test.
+ */
+NTSTATUS WINAPI NtFlushProcessWriteBuffers(void)
+{
+    static const long long drain_ns = 5000;
+    struct timespec start, now;
+
+    __atomic_thread_fence( __ATOMIC_SEQ_CST );
+    clock_gettime( CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &start );
+    do
+    {
+        __builtin_ia32_pause();
+        clock_gettime( CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &now );
+    } while ((now.tv_sec - start.tv_sec) * 1000000000LL + (now.tv_nsec - start.tv_nsec) < drain_ns);
+    return STATUS_SUCCESS;
+}
+
 #else /* __linux__ */
 
 /**********************************************************************
